  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Caliciopsis pinea is a rare fungal species in the Coryneliaceae family that forms a complex relationship with pine trees throughout New England. This ascomycete is notable for its small, cup-shaped fruiting bodies and its ecological role in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Produces distinctive small, dark ascomata (fruiting structures) with a characteristic cup or flask-like shape",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host association",
      "description": "Primarily found on dead or dying branches of pine species, particularly in areas with high moisture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic spores",
      "description": "Contains eight-spored asci typical of Coryneliaceae family members",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Caliciopsis pinea thrives on decaying pine wood and branches in the moist, temperate forests of New England, preferring areas with adequate humidity and shade.",
